      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "A remote Hispanic village, San Mateo evolved into a key religious hub where the Penitente Brotherhood kept a modest morada for worship.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/san_mateo_new_mexico",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Mid‑century, San Mateo joined the Grants Uranium Belt, earning the nickname “Uranium Capital of the World” after decades of intensive mining.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/san_mateo_new_mexico",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "San Mateo sits beside the San Mateo Creek Basin, while Mount Taylor—an 11,301‑ft stratovolcano sacred to Navajo, Acoma, and Hopi—casts cultural influence over the valley.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/san_mateo_new_mexico",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "San Mateo experiences a cold semi‑arid climate, with only about 11 inches of rain a year and abundant sunshine typical of the high‑desert Southwest.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/san_mateo_new_mexico",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "vague"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "At roughly 7,200 feet, San Mateo’s altitude keeps temperatures cooler than lower valleys, averaging a pleasant 45°F year‑round.",
